# Task 2 - Shared Responsibility Model Overview

The Shared Responsibility Model divides security responsibilities between the cloud provider and the customer. The customer’s responsibilities vary depending on the cloud service model.

## Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)

Operating an in-house data center or server facility is a complex undertaking. It demands total accountability for physical protection, hardware reliability, system patches, network configuration, and numerous other responsibilities. 

While every technology business requires computing resources to function, many lack the specialized skills or budget necessary to host and protect their own hardware safely. **Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)** addresses this exact challenge. It is a cloud delivery framework that offers virtualized computing assets over the internet whenever they are needed. 

By utilizing major IaaS platforms—such as Amazon AWS, Microsoft Azure, or Google Cloud—organizations can instantly spin up virtual servers through a simple web dashboard, eliminating concerns over hardware breakdowns and electrical disruptions.

## Platform as a Service (PaaS)

Certain organizations require more streamlined solutions than what IaaS provides. Software engineers, for instance, often prefer not to deal with setting up and configuring virtual servers. Instead, they want to focus purely on coding, deploy their work with a single click, and have their software go live seamlessly—without needing to manage the underlying hosting environment. 

**Platform as a Service (PaaS)** fulfills this need by offering a cloud environment designed specifically for easy web application development and deployment. 

Notably, platforms like TryHackMe incorporate PaaS functionality, allowing users to build and publish custom interactive rooms in the cloud without needing to understand the backend infrastructure. To power these services, TryHackMe itself relies on IaaS (specifically Amazon AWS) to run its virtual machines and core systems. Prominent examples of PaaS platforms include Heroku, Vercel, and Google App Engine.

## Software as a Service (SaaS)

**Software as a Service (SaaS)** enables individuals and organizations to access sophisticated cloud-hosted applications directly, eliminating the need for local software installations on personal devices. 

Countless applications fall under this category, including everyday tools like Slack, Zoom, Gmail, Google Docs, and Dropbox. Unlike IaaS or PaaS, SaaS delivers a fully completed end product ready for use by non-technical users right out of the box.

## Function as a Service (FaaS)

**Function as a Service (FaaS)**—often referred to as serverless computing—is a cloud computing service model that takes abstraction one step further than PaaS. 

While PaaS allows developers to deploy entire web applications without managing the underlying servers, FaaS lets developers deploy individual functions or pieces of code that run in response to specific events (such as an HTTP request, a file upload, or a database update).

